    <!-- ===================================================
             SECTION: THERMODYNAMIC PRINCIPLES
             =================================================== -->
    <hr class="p5-sep" />
    <div class="p5-section-label">Thermodynamic Foundation</div>
    <h2 class="p5-section-title">Real science: pressure-RPM coupling and load-following</h2>
    <p class="p5-section-lead">
        Phase 5 operates on the principle of dynamic pressure regulation. Unlike traditional
        engines where fuel flow is throttled, this system regulates steam pressure output
        proportionally to engine RPM demand. As throttle increases, the PCW opens the pressure
        relief valve partially, allowing more steam to enter the rails. This creates a
        thermodynamic equilibrium: <strong>Pressure (PSI) = k × Engine RPM (scaled)</strong>,
        where k is a calibration constant determined by tank capacity and desired acceleration curve.
    </p>

    <div class="p5-thermo-grid">
        <div class="p5-thermo-card">
            <div class="p5-card-label">Rankine Cycle Efficiency</div>
            <h4>Two-Phase Steam Expansion</h4>
            <p>
                Steam at pressure P₁ enters the dual rails. As it expands through the rail
                cylinders, it performs work on the piston rods (W = ∫P dV). For saturated steam
                at 100–150 PSI: η_carnot ≈ 12–18% (Rankine efficiency). Real-world Phase 5
                targets 22–28% through optimized expansion timing and heat recovery.
            </p>
            <p style="margin-top: 0.8rem; font-size: 11px; color: #3a5a6a; font-style: italic;">
                Reference: Clausius–Clapeyron equation; steam tables (NIST database)
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p5-thermo-card">
            <div class="p5-card-label">Pressure Regulation Curve</div>
            <h4>Load-Following Control Law</h4>
            <p>
                <strong>P(t) = P_min + (P_max − P_min) × [Throttle% / 100] × [RPM_current / RPM_max]</strong>
            </p>
            <p style="margin-top: 0.6rem;">
                Example: At 50% throttle and 2000 RPM (40% of max 5000 RPM):
                P = 20 + (150 − 20) × 0.50 × 0.40 = 20 + 26 = <strong>46 PSI</strong>
            </p>
            <p style="margin-top: 0.8rem; font-size: 11px; color: #3a5a6a;">
                Tank pressure never exceeds rated P_max, preventing over-pressure rupture.
                PCW samples throttle input at 100 Hz, adjusts valve orifice in real-time.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p5-thermo-card">
            <div class="p5-card-label">Piston Displacement & Flow Rate</div>
            <h4>Rail Volume & Steam Consumption</h4>
            <p>
                Each rail (double-acting cylinder): bore ∅80 mm, stroke 120 mm.
                Displacement per stroke: V = (π/4) × D² × L = (π/4) × (80)² × 120 ≈ 604 cm³
            </p>
            <p style="margin-top: 0.6rem;">
                At 3000 RPM (50 cycles/sec), total flow demand:
                Q = 2 rails × 604 cm³ × 50 Hz = <strong>60,400 cm³/s ≈ 60.4 L/s</strong>
            </p>
            <p style="margin-top: 0.8rem; font-size: 11px; color: #3a5a6a;">
                Tank rated for 25-gallon (94.6L) at 150 PSI max; supply valve sized for
                full-load demand with 15% margin.
            </p>
        </div>

        <div class="p5-thermo-card">
            <div class="p5-card-label">Heat Recovery & Exhaust</div>
            <h4>Condensation & Re-Pressurization</h4>
            <p>
                Exhaust steam exits the rail cylinders at ≈80 PSI, 212°F (saturated).
                A condenser/heat exchanger cools exhaust back to liquid water (L_vap = 970 BTU/lb).
                Recovered water returns to boiler reservoir; heating load supplied by Battery A.
            </p>
            <p style="margin-top: 0.6rem;">
                Heat duty: Q = ṁ × L_vap = (2 kg/s) × (2257 kJ/kg) ≈ <strong>4.5 kW</strong>
                (requires ≈360 A  12V from heating pack).
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>

